Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "Presents an RL framework for automated code refactoring that pre-trains a syntax-guided contrastive code-graph encoder and fuses embedding dynamics, code metrics, and differential tests into a composite reward; a GAT policy operates on joint representations. Experiments claim SOTA across several datasets and ablations show each component matters.", "significance_and_novelty": "Idea of using contrastive code-graph pre-training to shape an RL reward/policy for refactoring is potentially interesting, but novelty is undermined by missing technical detail and unclear baselines/datasets, making the true advance hard to verify.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Sec.4 introduces a coherent high-level recipe that couples contrastive graph embeddings with RL, which is a plausible way to balance syntactic and semantic refactoring signals.", "Sec.5.3 ablation table attributes gains to contrastive pre-training and semantic tests, suggesting each component contributes.", "Sec.5.5 reports a correlation between embedding movement and quality (\u2206h vs SI), hinting that the learned space captures refactoring-relevant structure."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Core equations are missing/garbled in Sec.3.2, Sec.4.1, Sec.4.2, and Sec.4.5 (e.g., contrastive loss and reward become \u2018\u0338\u2019 or blank after \u2018becomes:\u2019), making the method unambiguous and non-reproducible.", "The action space and code-edit operators are never specified in Sec.3.1/\u00a74, so it is unclear what refactorings are permitted, how graph transitions P are defined, or how validity constraints are enforced.", "Sec.4.1 claims \u2018subtree masking\u2019 and \u2018edge rewiring\u2019 preserve semantics but provides no algorithm or guarantees; such augmentations are likely to break behavior, undermining contrastive supervision.", "Sec.4.2 rewards embedding displacement \u2206h without justification; this may incentivize gratuitous large edits; integration with tanh and weights \u03b1,\u03b2,\u03b3 is undefined (no formula) and only a single ablation is shown (Tab.2).\",\"Sec.4.3\u2019s Mahalanobis-guided exploration is not specified mathematically nor how it plugs into PPO sampling, leaving exploration mechanics unclear.\",\"Related work and baselines in \u00a72.3/\u00a75 cite non\u2013peer-reviewed or future-dated works (e.g., \u2018Marvellous et al., 2025\u2019; \u2018Polu, 2025\u2019), misattribute \u2018GraphRL\u2019 (survey, not a method), and include the authors\u2019 own arXiv (Palit & Sharma, 2024b), breaching double-blindness and weakening comparisons.\",\"Datasets and metrics in \u00a75 are mismatched to refactoring (e.g., BigCloneBench for clone detection; \u2018CodeRef\u2019 appears to be RepoTransBench for translation), and SP relies on symbolic-execution-generated tests with no cost or coverage details; results lack variance/confidence and implementation detail, so claims are not verifiable.\",\"The paper exhibits numerous grammatical errors and unnatural phrasing (e.g., \u2018lemon deep learning\u2019, \u2018most often do last year\u2019), missing figures, and duplicated/erroneous sectioning (\u00a72 \u20182. Learning-based :\u2019), hurting clarity and credibility."], "suggestions": [], "rating": 3, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 1, "presentation": 1, "contribution": 2}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes an RL framework for automated code refactoring that leverages a syntax\u2011guided contrastive encoder over code graphs, fuses learned embeddings with code quality metrics in the reward, and uses a GAT policy with embedding\u2011guided exploration. Claimed results show SOTA across multiple datasets and cross\u2011language generalization, with ablations supporting the design.", "significance_and_novelty": "Combining contrastive code-graph pretraining with RL for refactoring and embedding-guided exploration is a plausible but incremental advance over prior contrastive code models and RL-based refactoring.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 4.1 proposes syntax\u2011aware contrastive pretraining over AST/CFG with reasonable augmentations (identifier shuffling, graph-level invariances).", "Section 4.2\u2019s composite reward that fuses code metrics, embedding dynamics, and unit-test gating is a coherent attempt to balance syntax and semantics.", "Section 4.3 introduces an embedding\u2011guided exploration via Mahalanobis prototypes that is an interesting, principled bias.", "Implementation Details provide concrete hyperparameters and training setup, aiding potential reproducibility."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Sections 4.1, 4.2, and 4.4 present key formulas only as placeholders (the actual contrastive loss, fused reward, and attention equations are missing), severely harming technical clarity and verifiability.", "Abstract and Introduction claim broad SOTA improvements, and Section 5.4 references Table 3, but no tables, numbers, or dataset/baseline specifics are available in the provided text to verify empirical claims.", "Section 4.1 labels subtree masking and CFG edge rewiring as \u201csyntax\u2011preserving\u201d without a mechanism or proof to guarantee semantic equivalence, raising correctness concerns for contrastive positives.", "Section 4.2 defines semantic preservation as \u03b4_t = I[test(G_t)=test(G_{t\u22121})], which compares only to the previous step rather than a fixed ground-truth suite, inviting reward hacking and not ensuring true semantic preservation."], "suggestions": [], "rating": 3, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 2, "presentation": 1, "contribution": 2}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes an RL framework for automated code refactoring that uses a syntax-guided contrastive encoder over code graphs to shape rewards, with a GAT policy operating on joint embeddings. It claims better refactoring quality, semantic preservation via differential testing, and cross-language generalization, supported by comparative results and ablations.", "significance_and_novelty": "Combining contrastive pretraining with RL for refactoring is a plausible direction and, if properly validated, could be impactful, but the paper does not convincingly establish novelty or provide sufficient technical detail to substantiate its contributions.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 5.3 ablation indicates the contrastive pretraining and semantic tests contribute materially to SI/SP, suggesting each component adds value.", "Section 4.5 introduces a differential-testing approach for semantic preservation, which is appropriate for the task.", "Section 5.4 attempts cross-language transfer, indicating awareness of generalization beyond a single language."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Throughout the paper (Abstract, Sections 1, 2.2, 3, 4.5) key technical details are missing or broken (no precise contrastive loss, augmentations, reward composition, action space, or RL algorithm specifics; the 4.5 formula is malformed), undermining soundness and reproducibility.", "Presentation is severely compromised by duplicated Abstract/Introduction text, typos and unnatural phrasing (e.g., \u201clemon deep learning,\u201d \u201cvariable-contrastive\u201d), and inconsistent sectioning/tables (\u201c3. RL-based :\u201d, ED metric undefined), as seen in Sections 1, 2.2, 5.2, 5.5, 6.3, 8.", "Empirical setup lacks crucial definitions of metrics (SI, SP, MG, GS, ED) and evaluation protocols; comparisons to PyLint/Cppcheck (Section 5.4) are misaligned with refactoring, casting doubt on claims.", "Related work and citations include inconsistent and seemingly non-credible entries with future years (e.g., Kupari et al., 2025; Marvellous et al., 2025), and misspellings (Syncobert) in Sections 2.2/2.3, weakening the novelty and positioning claims."], "suggestions": [], "rating": 3, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 1, "presentation": 1, "contribution": 2}
